                <h2>为什么本地化总是“看起来做了但没效果”</h2>
<p>很多出海团队把本地化等同于“翻译”——把英文广告词丢进 Deepl，检查一下语法就上线了。结果呢？点击率还行，转化率惨淡；用户评论全是“看不懂”“不感兴趣”“骗子”。</p>
<p>问题不在文案质量，而在本地化的完整度。下面这份清单来自多个项目踩坑复盘，涵盖东南亚与拉美两大主力市场，建议每次投放前逐项核对。</p>
<hr />
<h2>东南亚市场本地化检查清单</h2>
<h3>① 语言与表达层</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>机器翻译直发</strong>：Deepl/Google Translate 的直出文案在印尼语、泰语、越南语中经常出现主谓倒置或敬语缺失，会让用户感觉“像机器人说话”。</li>
<li><strong>未区分正式/非正式语体</strong>：泰国市场对不同年龄、身份的人说话语体差异很大，广告文案用错语体会被视为不礼貌。</li>
<li><strong>数字格式未本地化</strong>：印尼用句点做千位分隔（如 1.000.000），越南则用逗号。用错格式会让价格信息看起来很奇怪。</li>
<li><strong>货币符号遗漏或错误</strong>：印尼盾（IDR）面值很大，用户看到“100 IDR”会以为是错误价格，必须标注清晰。</li>
</ul>
<blockquote>
<p><strong>操作步骤</strong>：文案初稿完成后，找母语目标市场的外包翻译或本地合作伙伴做一轮“口语化润色”，重点改写 Call-to-Action 按钮文字。</p>
</blockquote>
<h3>② 视觉与文化层</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>用错手势或颜色</strong>：在泰国，头部被视为最神圣的身体部位，广告素材中要避免手指指向头部的画面。印尼部分地区对特定颜色有禁忌。</li>
<li><strong>人物肤色与审美不符</strong>：东南亚用户对美白广告已有明显抵触，若产品定位为美白类，需谨慎处理展示方式。</li>
<li><strong>未使用本地明星/KOL</strong>：在越南、印尼，本地KOL的带货效果远高于国际明星，本地化素材不用本地面孔会直接影响信任度。</li>
</ul>
<blockquote>
<p><strong>操作步骤</strong>：制作素材前，搜索目标市场近期热门的本地品牌广告截图，提取共同视觉元素作为参考基准。</p>
</blockquote>
<h3>③ 渠道与支付层</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>支付方式遗漏</strong>：越南市场 COD（货到付款）占比超过 70%，不做 COD 入口的电商广告转化率会大幅下降。</li>
<li><strong>平台选择错误</strong>：印尼用户主要活跃在 Instagram 和 TikTok，Facebook 反而渗透率不如预期。</li>
</ul>
<blockquote>
<p><strong>操作步骤</strong>：开户前确认目标市场的广告平台政策，部分金融类、博彩类产品在东南亚各国限制不同，需单独审核。</p>
</blockquote>
<hr />
<h2>拉美市场本地化检查清单</h2>
<h3>① 语言与表达层</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>西语未区分国家口音</strong>：阿根廷西语用 “voseo”（用 vos 代替 tú），墨西哥喜欢大量俚语，巴西则是葡萄牙语不是西语。用错变体会被用户调侃“装错了地方”。</li>
<li><strong>直译导致歧义</strong>：中文“物美价廉”直译成西语可能变成“便宜又难看”，这类错误会直接损害品牌可信度。</li>
<li><strong>标点与日期格式</strong>：巴西用 DD/MM/YYYY，墨西哥用 MM/DD/YYYY，在落地页表单里混用会造成用户注册错误。</li>
</ul>
<blockquote>
<p><strong>操作步骤</strong>：西语文案建议分国家定制，特别是 Argentina、Brazil、Mexico 三大市场，至少要有三个本地化版本。</p>
</blockquote>
<h3>② 节日与时间节点</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>错把中国节日当卖点</strong>：双十一、黑五在拉美市场影响力有限，真正的购物高峰是“Hot Sale”（墨西哥，5月）和“Black Friday”（巴西，11月）。</li>
<li><strong>忽视宗教节日</strong>：哥伦比亚、厄瓜多尔等天主教国家，复活节前一周是重要的消费沉默期，投促销广告效果极差。</li>
<li><strong>时区未对应用户活跃时间</strong>：拉美用户活跃高峰在当地时间晚上 8-11 点，凌晨上素材等于白投。</li>
</ul>
<blockquote>
<p><strong>操作步骤</strong>：制作“拉美市场年度营销日历”，提前规划每个国家的重点投放节点，避开当地法定假期和宗教节日。</p>
</blockquote>
<h3>③ 视觉与支付层</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>用美国素材直接翻译</strong>：拉美用户对美国品牌的“硬广感”接受度低，UGC 风格、真人测评类素材转化率明显更好。</li>
<li><strong>支付方式不匹配</strong>：巴西主流支付是 Boleto Bancário 和 Pix，墨西哥则是 OXXO 和 Mercado Pago，不用本地支付方式的电商广告流失率极高。</li>
<li><strong>货币标注混乱</strong>：巴西用户看到美元符号会本能跳出页面，必须使用本地货币 BRL 并标注清楚。</li>
</ul>
<blockquote>
<p><strong>操作步骤</strong>：在落地页底部添加当地主流支付方式的图标 logo，提升用户付款意愿。</p>
</blockquote>
<hr />
<h2>快速自检：本地化上线前必查这 5 项</h2>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>检查项</th>
<th>东南亚重点</th>
<th>拉美重点</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>语言本地化</td>
<td>母语审校 + 口语化</td>
<td>分国家西语版本 + 葡语</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>文化合规</td>
<td>手势/颜色/头部禁忌</td>
<td>宗教节日/肤色审美</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>支付方式</td>
<td>COD、OVO、DANA</td>
<td>Pix、Boleto、OXXO</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>节日节点</td>
<td>斋月、泰国新年</td>
<td>Hot Sale、黑五、复活节</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>素材风格</td>
<td>本地 KOL、接地气</td>
<td>UGC、测评、真实感</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<hr />
<h2>一句话总结</h2>
<p>本地化不是翻译的终点，是“让目标市场用户觉得这条广告就是为他们而发”的起点。东南亚看渠道和支付，拉美看语言变体和宗教节日——把这两句话贴在工位上，投放前逐项过一遍，至少能减少 80% 的常见坑。</p>
            </div>
            <div class="prose max-w-none" x-show="lang === 'en'" x-cloak>
                <h2>Why Localization Often Looks Done But Doesn't Work</h2>
<p>Many overseas teams equate localization with "translation" — putting English ad copy into Deepl, checking grammar, and launching. The result? Decent CTR, terrible conversion; user comments full of "can't understand" and "not interested."</p>
<p>The problem isn't copy quality — it's the completeness of localization. This checklist comes from project post-mortems across multiple markets, covering Southeast Asia and Latin America, and should be verified before every campaign.</p>
<hr />
<h2>Southeast Asia Localization Checklist</h2>
<h3>Language &amp; Expression</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>Publishing machine translations directly</strong>: Deepl/Google Translate output for Indonesian, Thai, and Vietnamese often has subject-verb inversions or missing honorifics, making users feel like they're "talking to a robot."</li>
<li><strong>Not distinguishing formal/informal register</strong>: Thai language has significant register differences for different ages and social positions; wrong register in ad copy is perceived as rude.</li>
<li><strong>Number formats not localized</strong>: Indonesia uses periods as thousand separators (e.g., 1.000.000), while Vietnam uses commas. Wrong format makes prices look strange.</li>
<li><strong>Currency symbols missing or incorrect</strong>: Indonesian Rupiah (IDR) has large denominations; users seeing "100 IDR" will think it's a pricing error — must be clearly marked.</li>
</ul>
<blockquote>
<p><strong>Action step</strong>: After the first draft, have a native-speaking freelance translator or local partner do a "colloquial polish" pass, focusing on Call-to-Action button text.</p>
</blockquote>
<h3>Visual &amp; Cultural</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>Wrong gestures or colors</strong>: In Thailand, the head is considered the most sacred body part — avoid pointing fingers at heads in ad creatives. Some Indonesian regions have color taboos.</li>
<li><strong>Skin tone and beauty standards mismatch</strong>: Southeast Asian users have clear resistance to whitening ads; if positioning as a whitening product, handle display carefully.</li>
<li><strong>Not using local celebrities/KOLs</strong>: In Vietnam and Indonesia, local KOLs drive sales far better than international stars — not using local faces in localized creatives directly impacts trust.</li>
</ul>
<blockquote>
<p><strong>Action step</strong>: Before creating materials, search for recent popular local brand ads from the target market and extract common visual elements as reference baselines.</p>
</blockquote>
<h3>Channels &amp; Payments</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>Missing payment methods</strong>: COD (Cash on Delivery) exceeds 70% in Vietnam — campaigns without COD options see drastically lower conversion rates.</li>
<li><strong>Wrong platform selection</strong>: Indonesian users are primarily active on Instagram and TikTok; Facebook penetration is actually lower than expected.</li>
</ul>
<blockquote>
<p><strong>Action step</strong>: Confirm ad platform policies for the target market before account setup — some finance and gambling products have different restrictions across Southeast Asian countries and require separate approval.</p>
</blockquote>
<hr />
<h2>Latin America Localization Checklist</h2>
<h3>Language &amp; Expression</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>Spanish not differentiated by country accent</strong>: Argentine Spanish uses "voseo" (vos instead of tú); Mexico loves slang; Brazil speaks Portuguese, not Spanish. Wrong variant gets users mocking "wrong place vibes."</li>
<li><strong>Direct translation causing ambiguity</strong>: Chinese "物美价廉" (good quality, low price) translated directly into Spanish might become "cheap and ugly" — such errors directly damage brand credibility.</li>
<li><strong>Punctuation and date formats</strong>: Brazil uses DD/MM/YYYY, while Mexico uses MM/DD/YYYY; mixing these on landing page forms causes user registration errors.</li>
</ul>
<blockquote>
<p><strong>Action step</strong>: Spanish copy should be customized by country — especially for Argentina, Brazil, and Mexico, prepare at least three localized versions.</p>
</blockquote>
<h3>Festivals &amp; Timing</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>Using Chinese festivals as selling points</strong>: Double Eleven and Black Friday have limited influence in Latin America; real shopping peaks are "Hot Sale" (Mexico, May) and "Black Friday" (Brazil, November).</li>
<li><strong>Ignoring religious festivals</strong>: In Catholic countries like Colombia and Ecuador, the week before Easter is an important consumption silence period — promotional ads perform terribly.</li>
<li><strong>Timezone not matching user peak times</strong>: Latin American users are most active 8-11 PM local time; launching creatives at midnight equals wasted spend.</li>
</ul>
<blockquote>
<p><strong>Action step</strong>: Create a "Latin America Annual Marketing Calendar" to plan key campaign nodes for each country in advance, avoiding local holidays and religious festivals.</p>
</blockquote>
<h3>Visual &amp; Payments</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>Translating American materials directly</strong>: Latin American users have low tolerance for "hard sell" American brand vibes; UGC-style and genuine review content converts significantly better.</li>
<li><strong>Payment methods not matching</strong>: Brazil's mainstream payments are Boleto Bancário and Pix; Mexico's are OXXO and Mercado Pago — e-commerce ads without local payment options have extremely high drop-off rates.</li>
<li><strong>Currency marking confusion</strong>: Brazilian users instinctively bounce when seeing dollar signs — must use local currency BRL and mark clearly.</li>
</ul>
<blockquote>
<p><strong>Action step</strong>: Add local mainstream payment method icons at the bottom of landing pages to boost user payment willingness.</p>
</blockquote>
<hr />
<h2>Quick Checklist: 5 Must-Verify Items Before Launch</h2>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Check Item</th>
<th>Southeast Asia Focus</th>
<th>Latin America Focus</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>Language localization</td>
<td>Native review + colloquial</td>
<td>Country-specific Spanish + Portuguese</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Cultural compliance</td>
<td>Gestures/colors/head taboos</td>
<td>Religious festivals/skin tone aesthetics</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Payment methods</td>
<td>COD, OVO, DANA</td>
<td>Pix, Boleto, OXXO</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Festival timing</td>
<td>Ramadan, Thai New Year</td>
<td>Hot Sale, Black Friday, Easter</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Creative style</td>
<td>Local KOLs, grounded</td>
<td>UGC, reviews, authentic feel</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<hr />
<h2>One-Line Takeaway</h2>
<p>Localization isn't the endpoint of translation — it's the starting point of "making target market users feel this ad was made specifically for them." For Southeast Asia, focus on channels and payments; for Latin America, focus on language variants and religious festivals. Tape these two phrases by your desk and run through the checklist before every campaign — this alone eliminates at least 80% of common pitfalls.</p>
